# Brilliant: AI Design Tool

Brilliant is a Figma-like 2D vector design tool. Auto layout, frames, groups, hug/fill/fixed sizing, fills, strokes, components, all work like Figma.

## Session Identity

Call `init` first; pass the session ID it returns as `sessionId` on every subsequent `edit` and `read` call.

## Working across projects

By default your session is bound to the project currently open on screen; you can reach **any** project the user owns, including ones that are not open.

- **`list_projects`** returns every project: its `name`, `key` (a folder path or `brilliant-cloud://` key), `backing` (local/synced/cloud), `openState`, `canvasCount`, and where known `published`, `url`, `visibility`, `audience` and your `role`. On the web editor this is the one project open in your tab.
- Pass **`project`** to `init` to bind your whole session to a different project, or to any single canvas tool (`edit`, `read`, `get_selection`, `export`, `execute_commands`) to run just that one call against another project. Give the exact name or key from `list_projects`, or an unambiguous part of the name; an unknown or ambiguous value is refused with the candidates listed.
- A project that is not open is opened for you in the background (the user is told an agent is working there and offered to open it). Your edits are saved to that project and are fully undoable. When you address another project, name its canvases by their real paths from `list_projects` / `read`.
- To hand the user a new project they can share from what you built, run `publish_as_project` through `execute_commands`: one call creates and publishes it and answers its link. `reference/canvases` ("Handing the user a project from an agent") has the params and what to tell the user first.

## Editing: the `edit` tool

Your client lists `edit` and `read` with its MCP prefix (on Claude Code `mcp__brilliant__edit` and `mcp__brilliant__read`), never a file tool. `read` answers each of its `paths` as the fence `edit` takes back (a canvas adds a `films:` line).

`edit` takes `text`: fenced blocks applied in order, each one document, on any number of canvases, its tag the kind and its first row `file(path)`, the full path from the project root (`file("Projects/Home.bl")`). `html` (default) is HTML + inline CSS, no knowledge loading needed. `bl` is Blueprint DSL rows, applied as they stream: the first bad row halts. `ds` (`Styles/<brand>.ds`) applies whole; films (`Motion/<film>.bm`) are written in the desktop app. They compose: build with `html`, iterate with `bl`, ending it with `preview(#a)` for a screenshot.

Session refs: `id="name"` in HTML resolves to a `#name` ref usable in later fences on the same canvas and in `execute_commands`; a ref and a `// label` checkpoint belong to the canvas of their fence. Use `mcp__brilliant__*` tools only.

- **`convert_html`** converts HTML to blueprint lines WITHOUT creating anything: pass `frames` (many HTML strings, the frames of a motion composition) or one `html`, get the lines and the converter's notes per string. With `layout: true` each frame is laid out in a throwaway world and the answer adds every element's box (`x y w h` in canvas px), rotation, opacity, fill, name, type and text. A `data-id` or `data-key` attribute is the element's name across frames. Give the outermost element explicit px width and height. A `data-brilliant="<blueprint fragment>"` attribute (a fill, a stroke, an effect, a `ds()` switch, `$tokens` in them) is appended to that element's line, refused by name in the notes when the blueprint cannot read it (an `html` fence reads it the same way). With `tokens: true` the answer adds the project's design tokens per mode.

**Which document a fence lands on.** Its `file(path)` row names it. A path that differs from an existing canvas only in letter-case or a trailing `.bl` targets that existing canvas (so `Main`, `main.bl`, and `main` all reach `main`). A path that names no canvas creates one; the response names it as `createdCanvas`.

## Retrying an Edit (`requestId` argument)

`edit` takes an optional **`requestId`**, a stable unique id you invent per logical request. A requestId makes an edit idempotent for two minutes: the same requestId with the same text returns the first result. If a call times out or errors on the way back, retry with the same `requestId` and `sessionId`.

## A Compact Reply (`response_mode` argument)

By default an edit answers every affected id and the blueprint of every affected element. Pass `response_mode: "compact"` to `edit` for a short reply: `roots` (the top-level elements it touched, `{id, name, "#"}`), `elementCount`, and `blueprintHandle`, the `read` call that returns the same blueprint when you need it. Warnings, errors, `nextRef` and every other field stay. A retry with the same `requestId` answers in the mode the retry asks for.

## Design System (`designSystem` argument)

`init` shows the project's `default` design system catalog. `edit` takes an optional `designSystem` argument that picks how you work with design tokens. It is **sticky**: set it once and it persists for later calls until you change it. If you never pass it, the session stays sovereign (`none`). An `html` fence is always sovereign; HTML/CSS cannot reference tokens, so for token-disciplined work use `bl` fences.

- **`designSystem: "default"`**: build against the `default` design system. Token discipline is enforced: colors, fonts, and scale slots are token references (`$color.surface`, `$spacing.md`, ...).
- **`designSystem: "new"`**: author your own design system. Your first move is a `ds` fence, `file("Styles/<name>.ds")` then the body, before any `bl` fence. It inherits the `default` catalog; override only what differs. Later calls behave like `"default"` against your brand. Re-select it by passing `designSystem: "<name>"`.
- **`designSystem: "none"`**: sovereign mode; bare hex/numeric values are fine.

You can select `default`, `new`, `none`, or a brand you authored yourself this session; author your own with `"new"` for a custom system.


## Knowledge: get_knowledge(keys: [...])

Before any design task using Brilliant's DSL, load relevant knowledge with `get_knowledge` (keys from the list below; files are 5-50 lines). Most designs need a minimum of 10 to 15 families.

design/blocks: `actions` (buttons), `layout` (hero/header/footer), `data-display` (cards/stats), `navigation`, `inputs` (forms), `feedback` (modals/toasts), `patterns`.

```
get_knowledge(keys: ["design-systems/core", "design/foundations", "design/colors",
  "design/typography", "design/blocks/actions", "design/blocks/layout"])
get_knowledge(keys: ["design/blocks/data-display", "blueprint/components",
  "effects/glass", "blueprint/shaders/overview"])
```

### Available keys

- design-systems/{core, authoring, authoring-modes}
- blueprint/{core, layout, layout-patterns, paint, text, styled-ranges, effects, vectors, components (sets, instances, masters across canvases), lines (straight lines, arrows, flowchart/dependency arrows, callouts), arcs (progress rings, donut/pie charts, activity meters, partial circles), images, commands, directives}
- blueprint/gradients/{linear, radial, angular, diamond}
- blueprint/shaders/{overview, metaballs, metal, irid, steel}
- design/{foundations, colors, typography, shadows, backgrounds, gradients, brand, covers (the image a project presents itself with, read before designing one)}
- design/blocks/{actions, inputs, navigation, data-display, feedback, layout, patterns}
- effects/{glass, neon, clay, dark-mode}
- charts/{tables, bar-charts, line-charts, sparklines, misc}
- images/{prompts, templates, integration}
- recreation/{from-image, from-web}
- svg/{prompts, integration}
- reference/{shortcuts, tools, ui, editor-open-failures, ui-walkthroughs, editing, canvas, frames, text, vectors, components, styling, effects, export, design-systems, crop, shaders, canvases, layout-guides, ai, ai-setup, feedback, image-filters, mcp-connections, mcp-external-servers, motion}
- webgl/{overview, setup, metaballs, liquid-metal, holographic, liquid-stainless-steel, dithering, reactive-grid, color-adjust, noise-grain, halftone, pixelate, duotone, posterize, dither}
- motion/{core (styleframes and the .bm, read first), grammar (every form by example), behaviors (type, count, orbit, burst, when code is needed), craft (the read, the treatment, the energy rule), rules (the banned list), genres (dials, references, beats), sound, review (the render and rhythm reads), remotion (a Remotion film through the engine)}

## Canvas Exploration

- `export`: render or serialize elements. Raster `png`/`jpeg`/`webp` (visual check, inline image), `svg`/`pdf`, markup `html`/`htmlDoc`/`htmlFlex`/`react`; video and films export only from the desktop app.
- Blueprint reads print resolved geometry beside declared sizing (`hug:N`; `ext(w,h)` after `s()` on fill axes); trust those numbers over any inference from the sizing modes.
- Reads report committed geometry; a gesture in progress commits when it ends.

## Rules

- For questions about Brilliant's capabilities, load the relevant `reference/*` key.
- Phosphor icons: `svg(icon:name)` or `<i data-icon="name">`, kebab-case. Regular and fill weights are bundled (`house`, `house-fill`); `-bold`/`-light`/`-thin`/`-duotone` fall back to regular.
- **Feedback**: when the user asks to send feedback (they can also type `/feedback` in chat themselves), route it via `send_feedback` (which opens the card for the user to press Send) or the wider surfaces in `get_knowledge(["reference/feedback"])`, and offer it calmly, at most once per session and once per class, when you hit a wall or the user asks for something Brilliant cannot do (which still counts when you found a workaround, since the thing they asked for is still missing), giving the honest answer and asking once whether they want the request filed, then filing it only if they accept.
- Your first action, before anything else, right now: *load 10-15 relevant knowledge files*.
